---
import Work from '../../layouts/Work.astro';
import BrowserFrame from '../../components/BrowserFrame.astro';
---

<Work
  title="Bottle Label Studio"
  category="Product · Engineering"
  summary="A browser tool that applies label artwork to bottle photography with perspective-aware warping and shading sampled from the photograph itself."
  description="Browser tool for compositing product labels onto bottle photos with perspective-aware warping and realistic shading. React, TypeScript and PixiJS, fully client-side."
  year="2026"
  role="Sole author — rendering approach and implementation"
  status="In internal use"
  stack={['React', 'TypeScript', 'PixiJS v8', 'Vite', 'Tailwind', 'zustand']}
  ogImage="/images/bottle-output.png"
  links={[{ label: 'View on GitHub', href: 'https://github.com/Takeshhii/bottle-label-studio' }]}
  schemaType="SoftwareApplication"
>
  <BrowserFrame
    src="/images/bottle-ui.png"
    alt="Bottle Label Studio interface with a label positioned on a bottle photograph and adjustment handles visible"
    url="bottle-label-studio"
    loading="eager"
    ratio="16 / 10"
  />

  <h2>The problem</h2>
  <p>
    Every time a label design changed, the options were a physical reshoot or manually
    warping and shading the artwork onto a bottle photograph in an image editor — per
    bottle, per variant. Neither scales while a design is still being iterated on.
  </p>

  <h2>The approach</h2>
  <p>
    Register a bottle once: upload a clean studio photograph, mark the four corners of
    the label area, set the curvature. After that, producing a new mockup is three
    steps — pick the bottle, drop in the artwork, export.
  </p>
  <p>
    The interesting part is making the result look printed rather than pasted. Two
    things do that work:
  </p>

  <h3>Perspective-aware warping</h3>
  <p>
    The label is mapped onto a subdivided plane anchored to the marked label area.
    Horizontally, columns compress toward the edges following the visible wrap angle,
    with a slight vertical bow so the top and bottom edges read like the ellipse you
    actually see on a cylinder.
  </p>

  <h3>Borrowed shading</h3>
  <p>
    Rather than hand-painting highlights and shadows per design, the tool samples the
    luminance already present in the bottle photograph across the label area,
    normalizes it, and multiplies it back over the warped artwork. Any label picks up
    that bottle's real highlights and shadows automatically, because it is reusing the
    photograph's own lighting rather than simulating new lighting.
  </p>

  <figure>
    <BrowserFrame
      src="/images/bottle-output.png"
      alt="Exported result: a label composited onto an aluminium bottle, with the metal's reflections showing through the artwork"
      url="exported result — full resolution PNG"
      ratio="4 / 3"
    />
  </figure>

  <h2>Implementation notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Everything composites at the source photograph's native resolution, so the export
      is as sharp as the input.
    </li>
    <li>
      Fully client-side — no backend, no accounts, no uploads. Bottle setups persist to
      local storage with JSON import/export for moving between machines.
    </li>
    <li>
      State is kept in a store with slice-level subscriptions rather than React context,
      because the canvas updates imperatively and dragging a corner should not
      re-render the component tree.
    </li>
    <li>
      Corner placement is recorded in photo-pixel space rather than screen space, so
      precision does not depend on how the canvas happens to be scaled.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Outcome</h2>
  <p>
    A label design becomes a usable product image in seconds, removing the manual
    mockup step for each new variant. The screenshots on this page are real exports
    produced by a script that drives the actual application headlessly, so they are
    reproducible from the repository rather than hand-picked.
  </p>

  <h2>Lessons</h2>
  <p>
    The borrowed-shading approach only works as well as the lighting in the source
    photograph — even studio light with the label area facing the camera gives the best
    result, and uneven light in the input shows up directly in the output. Getting the
    warp to read as a real cylinder rather than a bent flat image took several
    iterations on the compression curve.
  </p>
</Work>
